  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Notes from the Director</h3>
  <p>I am happy to report that Susan Clarke has joined the staff of Research Services as our new Grant Development Specialist. She succeeds Kelly Chris-topher, who is now an Associate Director in the Center for Experiential Learning.</p>
  <p>Susan has an MA in Experimental/Physiological Psychology from the University of Chicago and a BA in Psychology from Emory University. She has 15 years of research, administrative, and grant-writing experience, and has been successful in writing federal, state, corporate, and foundation grants. She comes to Loyola from The Harbour, Inc., a nonprofit social services organization. Susan will be a valuable addition to the ORS team.</p>
  <p>Susan has an MA in Experimental/Physiological Psychology from the University of Chicago and a BA in Psychology from Emory University. She has 15 years of research, administrative, and grant-writing experience, and has been successful in writing federal, state, corporate, and foundation grants. She comes to Loyola from The Harbour, Inc., a nonprofit social services organization. Susan will be a valuable addition to the ORS team.</p>
  <p>Please join me in welcoming Susan Clarke to Loyola and the ORS team!</p>
  <p style="text-align: right; margin-top: 40px;">—Bill Sellero</p>

  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Award Spotlights</h3>
  <p>The US Department of Education has awarded Loyola University Chicago a grant of <span data-field="award_amount_1">{{ award_amount_1 }}</span> toward improving undergraduate achievement. The five-year award, entitled “LUC Academic Support for Suc-cess,” will be directed by Provost John Peissero, PhD, and will provide enhanced academic and career development support to low-income, first-generation students and to students with disabilities. The goal of this program is to enhance retention, academic performance, graduation rates, and pursuit of post-baccalaureate education at risk undergraduate students. The first increment of <span data-field="award_amount_2">{{ award_amount_2 }}</span> was awarded in September 2010.</p>
  <p>The School of Law has received a four-year award in the amount of <span data-field="award_amount_3">{{ award_amount_3 }}</span> from the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation to develop a Master’s Degree Program in Rule of Law for Development at LUC’s Rome campus. This program will train 20-30 law graduates per year in advanced and advocacy for implementing and expanding rule of law in countries that are developing and/or in economic transition. Half of admitted students will be recruited from such countries. The program is expected to open in Spring 2011.</p>
  <p>Michael Mahar, PhD, Clinical Assistant Professor in the School of Education, has received a three-year award of <span data-field="award_amount_4">{{ award_amount_4 }}</span> from the US Agency for International Development for a project entitled “Empowering Cuban Entrepreneurs and the CSGs that Sustain Them.” This award, which is an outgrowth of a partnership between LUC and USAID since 2004, will allow Dr. Mahar and colleagues to expand their work in strengthening Civil Society Groups (CSGs) in Cuba. Cuban CSGs serve to provide not only the essential services of education and health care, but also the promotion of self-sufficiency and transition from poverty via training in the development of business, financial, and entrepreneurial skills. Training is particularly targeted to underserved populations (the poor, Afro-Cubans, and women). CSGs play a cru-cial role in improving the lives of the island population as Cuba moves toward a more open society.</p>
</div>

